<h1>Diverting &amp; Merging Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Diverting and merging conveyor systems route products between conveyor lines, splitting a single line into multiple destination lanes (diverting) or combining multiple infeed lanes into a single outbound line (merging). These systems are the traffic management layer of every <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> and distribution center, controlling product flow between processes, sort lanes, shipping destinations, and storage areas. Divert methods range from simple pneumatic pushers to high-speed <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="divert-heading">Divert Methods</h2>
<h3>Push Diverters</h3>
<p>Push diverters use pneumatic or electric actuators to push products off the main conveyor onto a perpendicular spur. Pneumatic pushers are the simplest and cheapest divert mechanism: a cylinder fires, a plate pushes the product, and the cylinder retracts. Cycle time (extend plus retract) limits how many products per minute a pusher can divert, and product weight and line speed set the cylinder size. They suit <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject systems</a>, simple two-way diverting, and low-speed applications. The pusher stroke is a pinch and impact point, so guard it per <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1, Safety Standard for Conveyors and Related Equipment</a>. Push diverters may rotate product orientation during the push.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Chain Transfers</h3>
<p>Pop-up chain transfers lift parallel chains above the <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> surface to drive products perpendicular to the original direction. The chains engage the product bottom and carry it to the spur conveyor. Pop-up chains maintain product orientation and handle heavier loads than wheel diverters, but the lift-transfer-lower cycle limits rate. They are the standard medium-speed divert mechanism in packaging and distribution. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyor guide</a> for detailed pop-up chain coverage.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Wheel Sorters</h3>
<p>Pop-up and pivot wheel sorters use small wheels that rise between belts or rollers, or pivot, to steer products onto an angled spur. The wheels provide a gentle divert that limits product rotation. <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/sortation/pivot-wheel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's belted pivot wheel sorter</a>, for example, places pivoting wheels at the infeed and exit belts of each divert. Wheel sorters are a common mid-to-high-rate choice for case and carton sorting in <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>, with rate and weight limits set by the specific model. Narrow belt sorters are a related design: <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/sortation/narrow-belt-sorter/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's ProSort MRT</a> pops up transfer rollers between narrow belts to divert at either 90° or 30°.</p>
<h3>Sliding Shoe Diverters</h3>
<p>Sliding shoe sorters use a slat conveying surface with a shoe on each slat. Selected shoes are switched to slide diagonally across the slats, gently pushing products off at an angle. The shoes create a smooth, controlled divert without impact or sudden directional change. <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/sortation/sliding-shoe-sorter/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol rates its sliding shoe sorters</a> at speeds up to 600 FPM, with 22° or 30° divert angles and models handling product up to about 350 lb (ProSort 1400LP). They are a core technology in e-commerce and parcel <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation</a>.</p>
<h3>Sweep Arm and Blade Diverters</h3>
<p>Sweep arm diverters use a pivoting arm or blade that swings across the conveyor to guide products onto a spur at a controlled angle. The arm sweeps smoothly rather than pushing abruptly, reducing product impact and rotation. Sweep diverters run at moderate speeds and loads. They are common in packaging lines where gentle product handling matters more than maximum speed.</p>
<h3>Tilt Tray and Crossbelt Diverters</h3>
<p>Tilt tray and crossbelt sorters are specialized high-speed divert systems where each product rides on an individual carrier. Tilt trays tilt to dump the product into the destination chute. Crossbelt carriers use a small belt that runs perpendicular to travel direction, pushing the product off the side. These systems handle the highest throughput and sort accuracy. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems page</a> for complete coverage.</p>
<h2 id="merge-heading">Merge Methods</h2>
<p>Merging combines products from two or more conveyor lines into a single outbound line without collisions, jams, or product damage. Merging is technically harder than diverting because two independent product streams must interleave precisely onto a shared line.</p>
<h3>Gap-Creation Merge</h3>
<p>The most common merge method creates gaps in the main line traffic to accept products from a spur. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor zones</a> on the main line slow or stop to create gaps. Products from the spur line enter the gaps. Photo-eye sensors detect products on both lines and PLC logic coordinates the gap creation and merge timing. Merge rate depends on product length, required gap, and conveyor speed.</p>
<h3>Zipper Merge</h3>
<p>Zipper merging alternates products from two infeed lines onto a single outbound line: one from line A, one from line B, one from line A, and so on. Each infeed line uses independently controlled zones that release products in alternating sequence. Zipper merges give high merge throughput because every gap on the outbound is filled. Zipper merges require equal or near-equal product flow from both infeed lines.</p>
<h3>Powered Merge Conveyor</h3>
<p>Powered merge conveyors use tapered or angled roller sections where the spur conveyor joins the main line at an angle. The angled merge section uses powered rollers running at a speed calculated to slot spur products into main line gaps. The merge angle and roller speed differential create a smooth entry without impact. Powered merges are usually part of the controls package that also runs upstream <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> and downstream sortation.</p>
<h3>Gravity Merge (Wye Merge)</h3>
<p>Gravity wye merges use two declining <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller conveyor</a> spurs that converge into a single outbound line in a Y-shape. Products from both spurs roll by gravity into the common outbound lane. Gravity merges have no collision control, products from both spurs may arrive simultaneously and jam. They suit low-volume manual operations where operators control the flow. Automated systems should avoid uncontrolled gravity merges.</p>
