<p>For multi-destination sortation, see our <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems guide</a>. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Diverting &amp; Merging Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line product routing:</strong> Diverters route products from a main transport conveyor to one of several packaging machines (case packers, labelers, sealers). Pop-up chain transfers or sweep diverters at each machine infeed activate based on PLC routing logic. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> upstream of each divert point buffer products for reliable divert timing.</p>
<p><strong>Quality reject systems:</strong> Push diverters at <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject stations</a> push failed products off the main line. Checkweighers, metal detectors, and vision systems trigger the divert. Push diverters provide the fastest cycle time and lowest cost for binary (pass/fail) reject decisions.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center sortation:</strong> High-speed divert systems route packages from induct conveyors to sort lanes based on destination data from barcode or RFID scanning. Pop-up wheel sorters and sliding shoe diverters handle the higher sort rates. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems page</a> for complete high-speed sortation coverage.</p>
<p><strong>Order fulfillment merging:</strong> Products picked from multiple zones merge onto a single pack-out conveyor. Each pick zone feeds a spur conveyor that merges with the main line through gap-creation merge zones. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR zone conveyors</a> provide precise merge control.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Collating and laning:</a></strong> Diverters distribute products from a single infeed across multiple parallel lanes for case packing, tray loading, or palletizer infeed. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/shuttle/">Shuttle conveyors</a> and reciprocating pushers handle multi-lane distribution at moderate throughput.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-line palletizer feed:</strong> Divert systems route cases from a common trunk line to one of several palletizer infeed lanes. Each palletizer builds a specific SKU pallet. The divert system reads case barcodes and activates the correct divert to route each case to its assigned palletizer.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Divert/Merge System</h2>
<p><strong>Speed and throughput are the primary selection drivers.</strong> Low divert rates: push diverter or sweep arm. Moderate rates with heavy or orientation-sensitive loads: pop-up chain transfer. High rates: pop-up wheel sorter, sliding shoe, or <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation system</a>. Give suppliers your peak diverts per minute and product length so they can confirm the rated rate of each option.</p>
<p><strong>Number of destinations determines system architecture.</strong> One or two destinations: single divert point. A handful of destinations: multiple divert points along a trunk line. Many destinations: dedicated sortation system with scanning, routing logic, and integrated divert array.</p>
<p><strong>Product characteristics affect divert method.</strong> Heavy products need pop-up chain transfers, heavy-duty shoe sorters, or <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">turntable transfers</a>. Light, flat-bottom products suit pop-up wheels and sliding shoes. Orientation-sensitive products require pop-up chains (no rotation) and prohibit push diverters (rotation risk).</p>
<p><strong>Merge design depends on infeed rates.</strong> Single spur merging into a low-traffic main line: simple gap-creation merge. Multiple spurs merging into a busy main line: zipper merge with coordinated zone controls. High-speed merge after sortation: powered merge conveyors with servo speed matching. Always provide enough <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> before merge points to buffer products during merge gaps.</p>
<p><strong>Scanning and controls integration.</strong> Automated diverting requires product identification (barcode, RFID, vision) and routing logic (PLC, WCS, or WMS). The scanning station must be far enough upstream of the divert point to allow processing time and product travel time. Controls latency, scanner read rate, and divert actuation time all affect the minimum distance between scan and divert.</p>
