<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a divert and merge system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A system that routes products between conveyor lines, splitting one line into multiple lanes (diverting) or combining multiple lanes into one (merging). Methods include push diverters, pop-up transfers, pop-up wheels, shoe sorters, and controlled merge zones. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types guide</a> for the full picture.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Diverting vs sortation, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Diverting routes products to a few destinations at a single decision point. <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">Sortation</a> routes to many destinations at high speed with scanning, routing logic, and multiple divert points. A diverter is a component; a sortation system is an integrated solution.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do merge conveyors prevent collisions?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Sensors detect products on both lines. The merge controller creates gaps in main line traffic by slowing upstream <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR zones</a>. Spur products enter the gaps. Zipper merges alternate products from each line for maximum throughput. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation</a> before merge points buffers products during merge timing gaps.</p>

</div>
</div>
